course: dictionary
lesson: 14506
title: Penalise: to punish or put at a disadvantage
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To penalise someone means to punish them for breaking a rule or law. «The referee penalised the player for a foul.» The player broke the rule, so the referee gave a punishment.
explain: A penalty can be a free kick, a fine or lost points. «Mia was penalised two marks for handing in her homework late.» Mia was punished with lost marks because her homework was late.
explain: Penalise can also mean to put someone at an unfair disadvantage, so they have a harder time than others. «The new rule could unfairly penalise children who live far from school.» Here nobody broke a rule. The rule itself makes some children's lives harder.
fact: Penalise is a verb. Its other forms are penalises, penalised and penalising.
fact: «Penalise» means to punish someone for breaking a rule or law.
fact: «Penalise» can also mean to put someone at an unfair disadvantage.
fact: A penalty can be a free kick in sport, a fine, or lost marks or points.
fact: Useful phrases: be penalised for, penalise heavily, unfairly penalised.
exercise: «The referee penalised the player for a foul.» What does penalised mean here? | Ignored by the crowd | Praised for playing well | Given a new ball | *Punished for breaking a rule
exercise: «Mia was penalised two marks for handing in her homework late.» Why was Mia penalised? | She finished first in the race | She got every answer right | *She handed in her homework late | She lost her pencil case
exercise: «The new rule could unfairly penalise children who live far from school.» What does unfairly penalise suggest? | Helps them to win prizes | Makes the school bigger | Means the rule is kind and fair | *Puts them at an unfair disadvantage
test: Yesterday the teacher ___ Tom for talking during the test. | *penalised | penalises | penalising | penalise
test: «The shop was penalised with a fine for selling out-of-date food.» What does penalised with a fine mean? | *Punished with money to pay | Closed for a holiday | Given a free gift for good service | Congratulated by the council
exam: Which sentence uses penalise to mean being treated unfairly? | The sun came out after the rain | The referee blew the whistle to start the match | *The rule unfairly penalises the poorest families | The baker made a big birthday cake
